  "text": "The tide came in an hour early. Maren stood on the slick basalt steps of the lower jetty, her lantern held high against the sea fret, watching dark water swallow the third iron ring. According to her tables—and the moon, which was still a pale, lopsided fingernail barely clear of the eastern headlands—the flats should have been bare mud for another fifty minutes.\n\nShe knelt, dipping two fingers into the swell. It wasn’t the numbing, brine-heavy cold of the northern reach. It was tepid, almost blood-warm, and carried a sharp, metallic tang like a mouthful of copper pennies.\n\nDown the coast, the bell buoy at Rook’s Teeth began to clatter, though there was no wind to rock it. The water wasn't rolling in; it was simply rising, smooth as oil filling a cistern, drowning the salt-marsh sedge without a whisper of surf.\n\nSomething bumped against the stone beneath her boots. It had the dull, hollow ring of heavy timber, yet when she lowered the lantern, she saw the gleam of tarnished brass tangled in the bladderwrack. Reaching in up to her elbow, she hauled it onto the dry steps. It was an armillary sphere, the size of a gull's cage, its interlocking rings etched with constellations she didn't recognize. Inside the nested bands sat a globe of thick, greenish glass, half-full of seawater. As she wiped the silt from its equator, something inside the glass clicked—a slow, deliberate escapement ticking against the wrongness of the tide."
}

