  "text": "Maren carried the dripping contraption up the spiraling iron stairs to the watch room, her boots leaving dark, copper-smelling crescents on the grates. She set the sphere on her charting table beside her dividers and tide ledgers. Up here, just beneath the great Fresnel lens, the ticking inside the green glass sounded louder, competing with the clockwork drive of the lamp above.\n\nShe took a rag to the outer meridian ring. The brass wasn't corroded by barnacles, only filmed in black silt that wiped away to reveal silver inlays. Instead of the Zodiac, the bands were marked with depth soundings in fathoms and strange, barbed glyphs. A small thumb-screw sat at the zenith.\n\nHolding her breath, Maren turned the screw a quarter-inch. \n\nThe escapement inside stuttered, then whirred. Within the trapped seawater, a filament of pale amber light ignited. The nested rings spun on greased gimbals, locking into alignment, and the glass globe cast a sharp, refracted shadow across the whitewashed walls of the room. \n\nIt was a bathymetric chart of the Oakhaven trench, rendered in razor-thin lines of light. Maren leaned in, her heart hammering against her ribs. The familiar shoals and Rook’s Teeth were there, but deep in the abyssal plain, a geometric mass was rising—an inverted spire of light that moved upward in time with the ticking mechanism, pushing the ocean before it like a piston.\n\nThree heavy, deliberate knocks echoed from the oak door at the base of the tower. Outside, the causeway was already six feet underwater."
