  "text": "Maren’s hand went to the iron gaff hook mounted by the doorframe. Above her, the amber projection of the trench spun slowly across the plaster, its ticking steady as a pulse. The knocks came again—not the frantic pounding of a shipwrecked sailor, but the dull, metallic thud of brass against oak.\n\nShe descended the spiral stairs, the warm, coppery dampness thickening with every turn. At the bottom, dark water was already seeping beneath the threshold, hissing faintly against the flagstones. Maren braced her boot against the lower frame, threw the heavy iron bolt, and hauled the door inward.\n\nThe surge rushed over her ankles, warm as blood. Standing on the drowned steps, chest-deep in the black swell, was a figure clad in a heavy canvas diving dress. \n\nThere was no boat in the boiling surf, and no air-hose or lifeline trailed from the bulbous brass corselet. Barnacles and pale, blind tubeworms crusted the soldered seams of the helmet. Behind the thick, cross-barred faceplate, no human breath fogged the glass. Instead, pale eyes with rectangular, goat-like pupils stared out from a face the color of tallow.\n\nA gauntlet of articulated brass rose from the water, dripping black silt. A small diaphragm on the helmet’s collarbone vibrated, producing a voice that sounded like pebbles grinding in an undertow.\n\n\"You turned the zenith screw, Keeper,\" the figure rasped, stepping over the threshold as the water rose to Maren’s shins. \"The ballast is rising. Give me back the plumb-line, or your tower goes under before the third bell.\""
